course: dictionary
lesson: 14658
title: Formal words at the start
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to formal writing, the careful, stiff voice of books, exams and official speeches. Prefatory is one of them. It describes something that comes at the beginning as an introduction. «The book opened with a prefatory note from the author.» The note comes first, before the story, so prefatory means introducing.
explain: Look for clues in the word. Prefatory comes from preface, the short introduction at the front of a book. So a prefatory part is the starter, and the main part comes after it. Ask yourself: is this the starter, before the main part?
explain: Everyday English would say introductory or opening instead. «The head teacher made some prefatory remarks before the sports day races began.» The remarks are a short start, and the races are the main event. Swap in a plainer word and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Prefatory means coming at the beginning as an introduction.
fact: A preface is a short introduction at the front of a book.
fact: «Prefatory remarks» are the short opening words of a speech, before the main talk.
fact: Formal words like prefatory are used in books, exams and official speeches.
fact: Introductory and opening mean much the same as prefatory, but they are less formal.
exercise: «The author wrote a prefatory note at the front of the book.» What does prefatory mean? | *Coming at the beginning as an introduction | Coming at the end as a conclusion | Written in a very old language | Hidden in the middle of the story
exercise: Which word means nearly the same as prefatory? | *Introductory | Hidden | Final | Forgotten
exercise: «The prefatory words were short, so the main speech could begin.» Where do the prefatory words come in the speech? | In the middle, after the main speech | *At the beginning | At the end | Nowhere at all
test: «The prefatory chapter explained how the characters first met.» What does prefatory mean here? | Colourful | *Introductory, coming at the start | Final, coming at the end | Very old
test: Why might a writer use the word prefatory instead of opening? | *It sounds formal, as in a book or official speech | It means the same as happy | It means the end | It is a fun word for games
exam: «Her prefatory note made the report clear before the figures began.» What is the best meaning of prefatory? | Angry | Final | *Introductory | Hidden
